## Proof

**Given:** Any group of 98 people can always be partitioned into 49 special relation pairs (a perfect matching of special relations always exists on 98 people).

**Claim:** The minimum number of disjoint special relation pairs that can always be found among 99 people is **49**.

### Upper Bound: At most 49 pairs

Since 99 is odd, any collection of mutually disjoint pairs can contain at most ⌊99/2⌋ = 49 pairs. Forming 50 disjoint pairs would require 100 people, which exceeds 99. Therefore, no configuration of 99 people can ever yield more than 49 disjoint special relation pairs.

### Lower Bound: At least 49 pairs always achievable

Given any arrangement of 99 people, select any one person and remove them. The remaining 98 people satisfy the given condition: they can **always** be divided into 49 special relation pairs. These 49 disjoint pairs are pairs among the original 99 people (removing one person does not affect the special relations among the remaining 98).

Therefore, in every possible configuration of 99 people, we can always find at least 49 disjoint special relation pairs.

### Conclusion

The lower bound (49, always achievable) equals the upper bound (49, the maximum possible for an odd number of people). Therefore, the minimum number of special relation pairs that is always guaranteed among 99 people is exactly **49**.

### PROOF COMPLETE
